    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1279aa1886e14cb880d74b81be8630f6",
      "statement": "Lease recovery is a single conditional `UPDATE` in `JobStore.recover_stale`: it requeues every row in `fetching`/`generating`/`finalizing` whose `last_seen_at` is `NULL` or older than `lease_seconds`, clears `claimed_by`/`claim_id`/`claimed_at`/`last_seen_at` and `cancel_requested`, and preserves `attempts` — which makes the call idempotent and safe to run from several API replicas.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/core/storage.py#L243-L269",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:25fdf753945a0b032263a0bb88cf92fc75616b0a43aca9352feab12d8ad2e0f7:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/core/storage.py#L345-L356",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0bc515054cd31cc2129d901d5d9de545e5d0a821001c26e4b6920b65148e00f1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_eca50ebf61874dce96899378a9f34a26",
      "statement": "`recover_stale` has exactly two triggers in the API process, both in `app/main.py`: once inside the `create_app` lifespan startup (after `purge_expired`, before the reaper task starts) and then every `WORKER_REAP_SECONDS` from the `openwiki-reaper` task created by `_reap_loop`, whose body also calls `prune_workers()` and is wrapped in `contextlib.suppress(Exception)` so the periodic requeue never dies on a transient error.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/main.py#L32-L40",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8d12b2cd359a8db5a6bd6ea33e07ab6c2998440ed681b6768e630e28770b27ee:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/main.py#L53-L60",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:689b5bcaf83ebdd3747a757b019e06916ab2c8d255c61e51d4a6bb7fb26b6a55:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_0673517202d04411b7722b7f8c2b38a6",
      "statement": "Recovery timing is controlled by `WORKER_LEASE_SECONDS` (default 180, the age of `last_seen_at` at which an orphaned claim is requeued), `WORKER_REAP_SECONDS` (default 30, the sweep period) and `WORKER_PROGRESS_SECONDS` (default 2.0, the heartbeat period and therefore the effective lease refresh rate); the intended relationship is `WORKER_LEASE_SECONDS >> WORKER_PROGRESS_SECONDS`, which allows roughly ninety missed heartbeats before a worker is declared dead and makes the worst-case detection latency of a hard-killed worker about lease plus reap interval.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/core/config.py#L49-L64",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:dfb022fc84d66dcf2bca09fb5903944f37446afaccc41cc745a6ab884752f9d6:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/worker/loop.py#L132-L157",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:9e8fc0c32d663a5670d96262684f3b351e0b87b20f4d44efefe6c18e5e28d9dd:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_656a169d2adb4dac848d627bb94bd7be",
      "statement": "A lost lease is terminal for that attempt because `claim_id` is the ownership token: `heartbeat` returns `{\"ok\": False, \"cancel\": True}` without writing when the pair no longer exists or the row left `RUNNING_STATUSES`, and `complete` only updates `WHERE wiki_id=? AND claim_id=?` so it returns `False`; `pipeline.report()` converts `ok=False` into `PipelineCancelled(\"the worker lease was lost\")` (closed as `cancelled`, not `failed`), and `_run` never checks the boolean returned by `complete` because the terminal state belongs to the holder of the live claim.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/core/storage.py#L271-L308",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c0451c6dd807cded94d8ca706ded480fd6d1db5ae09ef36202e437e1669ec677:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/core/storage.py#L310-L343",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c540bace3fc1d460fd6018a3a97eba92c688a686bf8ea90a140044f4f36aa18c:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/worker/loop.py#L96-L105",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:76c7b0a68be02e6a13e71d03619be89852f6ac2f169927b1d1f39db349d73d0d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_0f818b4d2252489799d6b7c20e0d809d",
      "statement": "A worker that is shut down or killed writes no terminal state at all: `_run` re-raises `asyncio.CancelledError` for a graceful worker shutdown (killing the CLI subprocess on the way out so no orphan process survives) and a SIGKILLed process simply disappears, so the row keeps its running status until `recover_stale` requeues it — which is what makes `docker compose restart worker` safe.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://app/services/wiki_runner.py#L256-L261",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6de50395fc82f8915b708ee3baa018b6b7f9b62727bb256db33ed03e994b726c:"
        },
